 \subsection{Carrier and Burst Timing}
 
 Both targets are configured for a fixed \SI{1}{\mega\hertz} carrier and nominal \SI{40}{\nano\second} dead interval. The G071 uses center-aligned TIM1 CH1/CH2 compare spacing. The F410 uses CH1/CH1N with the TIM1 dead-time unit, leaving PA9 free for USART1. ARR sets the timer range, CCR1 the output compare point, and DTG the programmed
-dead time. With a \SI{100}{\mega\hertz} timer clock, the V2 settings are ARR \(=50\), CCR1 \(=25\), DTG \(=4\), and repetition count \(=1\). CH2 is disabled. The stated dead time is calculated from registers; gate-driver propagation and transistor switching still need measurement.
+dead time. With a \SI{100}{\mega\hertz} timer clock, the V2 settings are ARR \(=50\), CCR1 \(=25\), DTG \(=4\), and repetition count \(=1\). CH2 is disabled. The programmed dead time is calculated from the timer registers. Actual gate timing and transistor commutation still need to be measured.
 
 In the enable scheduler, \texttt{FEN} and requested duty are converted to a period, enabled interval, and pulse-skip count. The period is rounded to microseconds. A complete minimum burst and minimum low interval are enforced, with a longer period if both cannot fit. Below the minimum burst energy, periods are skipped; bursts are not shortened. With a permitted static \SI{100}{\percent} command, enable stays high.
 
